<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1632–<a href="/years/1688">1688</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Narai the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narai was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> from 1656 to 1688. He opened Siam to wide foreign contacts, exchanging embassies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and employing the Greek adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-phaulkon">Constantine Phaulkon</a>, and he made Lopburi a second royal residence. His forces destroyed the rebellious sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songkhla">Singora</a> in 1680. His death was followed by the revolution of 1688 and the expulsion of the French.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narai.jpg" alt="Narai" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clestur at English Wikipedia · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narai.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Prince Narai was a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasat-thong">Prasat Thong</a>, who had usurped the throne in 1629 and founded the Prasat Thong dynasty, and of Princess Sirithida, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtham">Songtham</a>. The Royal Chronicle says the infant was seen to have four arms before having two, and that the king took this as a miracle and named him Narai, from Narayana, a name of the four-armed god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. After Prasat Thong's death in 1656 his son Prince Chai became King Sanphet VI, but Prince Si Suthammaracha plotted with Narai to bring him down, and Sanphet was executed after nine months.</p><p>Si Suthammaracha took the crown and made Narai viceroy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-palace">Front Palace</a>. The two soon fell out. Narai drew on Persian and Japanese mercenaries and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, and on the Day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a> they stormed the palace. Si Suthammaracha was captured and executed on 26 October 1656.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Narai's reign was the most prosperous of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> period, with great commercial and diplomatic activity with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and the West. In the Burmese–Siamese War of 1662 to 1664 he brought Chiang Mai under direct control, led an army of 60,000 into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and besieged Pagan in 1664 before retreating. He built a new palace at Lopburi with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> architects and engineers. His destruction of the Sultanate of Singgora came in 1680.</p><p>The Greek adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-phaulkon">Constantine Phaulkon</a>, introduced at court in 1681, became Narai's closest counsellor and arranged the growth of French influence: French troops were stationed in Mergui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>. Embassies went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1680, 1684 and 1686, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1688. Narai showed interest in both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, but there is no concrete evidence he wished to convert to either.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Narai fell gravely ill at Lopburi in March 1688. On 18 May Phra Phetracha, commander of the Royal Regiment of Elephants and the leading opponent of the French, seized the palace. Phra Pi, whom Narai had adopted as successor, was executed on 20 May and Phaulkon on 5 June. Narai died on 10 or 11 July, and Phetracha proclaimed himself king. He besieged the French troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, and after four months and a negotiated settlement they were allowed to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>Narai is counted among eight Thai monarchs given the title the Great. The accounts of French, Persian and other visitors give a rare view of the Ayutthayan court, since most original records were destroyed with the city in 1767, and Thai literature flourished in his reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Narai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Literature in Thai, including court poetry and chronicles, flourished especially under King <b>Narai</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Officials took it too: the Kalahom Prasat Thong in 1629, and Phetracha, <b>Narai</b>’s elephant commander, in 1688.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mughal general Asaf Khan invaded in 1564, and Durgavati died in battle near <b>Narai</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/garha-kingdom">Garha Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Lopburi remained a secondary royal city, used by <b>King Narai</b> as a residence.</blockquote><a href="/state/lavo-kingdom">Lavo Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Sultan Mustafa, <b>King Narai</b> of the Ayutthaya Kingdom sent a large force that destroyed Singora in 1680.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-singora">Sultanate of Singora</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Phra Ratchawang Boran, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in King Narai's Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.36,100.56&amp;year=1668" aria-label="Phra Ratchawang Boran on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7301 1629 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Narai"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7751 1899h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7751 1899h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.36,100.56&amp;year=1668">What was here in 1668? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
